# [GROW](../wg/grow.html)

## Summary

The Global Routing Operations (GROW) Working Group met to discuss several key protocol extensions, data models, and operational guidelines for the BGP Monitoring Protocol (BMP). Key topics included working group governance and gatekeeping initiatives (requiring running code and consolidation of competing drafts), progress on BMP v4 (`draft-ietf-grow-bmp-tlv`), updates to the BMP YANG model (`draft-ietf-grow-bmp-yang`), event framework integration (`draft-ietf-grow-bmp-rel`), route filtering (`draft-ietf-grow-bmp-adj-ribs-filtered`), statistical information TLVs (`draft-ietf-grow-bmp-stats-informational-tlv`), and several proposals for monitoring BGP message counts, Loc-RIB metadata, and RPKI processes.

## Key Discussion Points

### Working Group Governance & Running Code
- **Presentation**: [Organizing GROW: forms & running code (chair slides)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-grow-organizing-grow-forms-running-code-chair-slides-00)
- **Presenters**: Paolo Lucente, Job Snijders
- **Discussion**:
  - Paolo Lucente outlined initiatives to consolidate competing frameworks, aggregate small proposals, manage protocol complexity, and introduce a "Project Kickoff Form" checklist before draft adoption.
  - While AI/LLM tools can assist specification writing, the chairs emphasized raising the bar for adoption to focus on realistic, implementable proposals rather than theoretical specifications.
  - Thomas Graf proposed forming a design team for BMP v5 to establish scope, guidance, and structure for future protocol extensions.
  - Job Snijders presented on requiring running code (at least two independent, interoperable implementations covering the specification) prior to progressing standards-track documents to the IESG.
  - Ahmed Elhassany emphasized that reported implementations must align with the current version of a draft to avoid fragmentation from pre-draft vendor variants.

### BGP Message Counters for BMP Stats Report
- **Presentation**: [01 - BGP Message Counters for BGP Monitoring Protocol (BMP)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-grow-01-slides-draft-choudhary-grow-bmp-bgp-msg-stats-ietf127-00)
- **Presenter**: Asim
- **Discussion**:
  - Asim presented a proposal to add 10 new stat types (64-bit counters for update/total BGP PDUs and 32-bit counters for Keepalive, Notification, and Route Refresh PDUs in both directions) to monitor liveness, churn, flapping, and route refresh storms over a single BMP feed.
  - Camilo Cardona questioned using BMP stats versus YANG models. Asim and Jeff Haas noted that having stats available over the unified BMP feed benefits specific operational ecosystems, provided counters are aligned with YANG (`draft-ietf-grow-bmp-yang`) and MIB definitions.
  - Jeff Haas suggested refining counters to support per-AFI/SAFI tracking for finer churn granularity.

### Merge of BMP Aggregated Route Monitoring and Common Updates
- **Presentation**: [02 - Update on the proposed merge of BMP Aggregated Route Monitoring and Common Updates](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-grow-02-update-on-the-proposed-merge-of-bmp-aggregated-route-monitoring-and-common-updates-00)
- **Presenters**: Yisong Liu, Mukul Srivastava
- **Discussion**:
  - Yisong Liu presented the merged approach combining multi-peer headers and RIB view compression (Common Update TLV) into a single aggregated route monitoring message to reduce wire overhead and speed up collector convergence.
  - Thomas Graf and Mukul Srivastava discussed constraining this mechanism strictly to BMP v4 (or future BMP v5) rather than maintaining dual wire formats for BMP v3 and v4.
  - Paolo Lucente recommended further consolidation before calling for Working Group adoption.

### Proposed BMP Event Framework Merge (GEN and REL)
- **Presentation**: [03 - (Tentative) Directional Update on the proposed BMP Event Framework merge (GEN and REL)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-grow-03-tentative-directional-update-on-the-proposed-bmp-event-framework-merge-gen-and-rel-00)
- **Presenter**: Camilo Cardona
- **Discussion**:
  - Camilo Cardona provided an update on merging General Event Notification (GEN) into Logging of Routing Events in BMP (`draft-ietf-grow-bmp-rel`).
  - `draft-ietf-grow-bmp-rel` will define the core event framework (event scoping, timestamps, common headers), while GEN will serve as the first application built upon this framework.
  - Clarified in response to Mukul Srivastava that the event framework will target BMP v4.

### BMP Statistics Information TLV
- **Presentation**: [04 - BMP Statistics Information TLV](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-grow-04-bmp-statistics-information-tlv-00)
- **Presenter**: Mukul Srivastava
- **Discussion**:
  - Mukul Srivastava presented updates to `draft-ietf-grow-bmp-stats-informational-tlv`, which augments BMP stats reports with distributional data (min, max, average, median, p5, p95 percentiles) over a configured measurement window.
  - Recent draft updates include an AFI/SAFI flag bit, explicit measurement window and sample count fields, and 8-byte timestamps.
  - In response to Thomas Graf, Mukul clarified that timestamps in this document sit inside the stats data payload specifically for min/max sample observation windows.
  - The authors requested Working Group Last Call (WGLC). Paolo Lucente noted the request and confirmed author plans for implementation.

### Monitoring RPKI-Related Processes Using BMP
- **Presentation**: [05 - Requirements for Monitoring RPKI-Related Processes on Routers Using BMP](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-grow-05-requirements-for-monitoring-rpki-related-processes-on-routers-using-bmp-00)
- **Presenter**: Shuwei Wang
- **Discussion**:
  - Shuwei Wang detailed requirements for reporting how RPKI ROA changes impact BGP route selection and proposed an RPKI ROA impact event message.
  - Jeff Haas cautioned against complex state-change inferences that strain router pipelines and recommended tracking aggregate statistics.
  - Paolo Lucente and Thomas Graf suggested expressing these event notifications through the generic event framework (`draft-ietf-grow-bmp-rel`).
  - Job Snijders recommended refining RPKI data source definitions (e.g., RPKI-to-Router protocol vs. static config), expanding scope to include ASPA alongside ROAs, and addressing scalability concerns.

### A YANG Data Model for BMP
- **Presentation**: [06 - A YANG Data Model for BMP](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-grow-06-a-yang-data-model-for-bmp-00)
- **Presenter**: Camilo Cardona
- **Discussion**:
  - Camilo Cardona presented updates to `draft-ietf-grow-bmp-yang` (version -10), addressing YANG doctor review comments and changing the normative dependency on `draft-ietf-grow-bmp-tcp-ao` to an informational reference.
  - Jeff Haas and Job Snijders advocated progressing the YANG model toward publication to encourage broader vendor implementation.
  - Authors plan to resolve remaining editorial items before requesting WGLC.

### Filtering Adj-Rib-In and Adj-Rib-Out to BMP Receivers
- **Presentation**: [07 - Filtering Adj-Rib-In and Adj-Rib-Out to BMP Receivers](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-grow-07-filtering-adj-rib-in-and-adj-rib-out-to-bmp-receivers-00)
- **Presenter**: Camilo Cardona
- **Discussion**:
  - Camilo Cardona covered updates to `draft-ietf-grow-bmp-adj-ribs-filtered`. Filtering criteria will point to YANG models rather than being encoded directly in BMP.
  - Based on working group feedback and real-world behavior (e.g., Junos), the draft will relax the strict requirement for Peer Down/Peer Up cycles on filter policy changes, allowing implementations to send delta route monitoring updates instead.

### BMP Loc-RIB Peer Address & BMP Local Path-ID
- **Presentation**: [08, 09 - BMP Loc-RIB: Peer Address, BMP Local Path-ID](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-grow-08-09-bmp-loc-rib-peer-address-bmp-local-path-id-00)
- **Presenter**: Maxence Younsi
- **Discussion**:
  - Maxence Younsi proposed encapsulating a Per-Peer Header directly inside a TLV for Loc-RIB route monitoring messages, alongside a companion Interface TLV for link-local peering context.
  - Jeff Haas suggested using an interface index instead of interface name for density and efficiency.
  - Maxence presented a revived proposal to combine Peer ID and Path ID into a single "Local Resource ID" TLV to track path state across RIBs.
  - Paolo Lucente supported renaming the draft to reflect local resource IDs and encouraged developing a companion IPFIX document for control/traffic plane correlation.

### BMP v4: Extended TLV Support
- **Presentation**: [10 - BMP v4: Extended TLV Support for BGP Monitoring Protocol (BMP)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-grow-10-bmp-v4-extended-tlv-support-for-bgp-monitoring-protocol-bmp-00)
- **Presenter**: Paolo Lucente
- **Discussion**:
  - Paolo Lucente presented updates to `draft-ietf-grow-bmp-tlv`, clarifying wording on indexing mechanisms across complex BGP PDU updates, withdrawals, and multi-protocol capabilities.
  - Working Group Last Call was issued prior to the meeting.

### Granular BMP Statistics Frameworks
- **Presentation**: [11 - Three BMP Statistics Drafts, One Shared Problem](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-grow-11-three-bmp-statistics-drafts-one-shared-problem-00)
- **Presenter**: Soumya
- **Discussion**:
  - Soumya discussed proposals extending BMP statistics to cover address-family-specific metrics (e.g., EVPN EVI/VPN instances, Route Distinguishers, and policy/attribute-impacted route counts).
  - Paolo Lucente requested that the authors post their specific questions regarding configuration, operational state, and structure to the GROW mailing list.

